{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/pair-mapping-validation.md", "content": "# Pair-mapping validation for split panels

When the input has two noisy panels separated by a divider and the output is smaller, treat aligned positions in the panels as paired observations. From every demo, build a full contingency table `(left_value, right_value) -> output_value`, not a handful of examples. Check consistency for every observed pair, note any exceptions, and ensure output dimensions correspond to one panel's aligned positions. This is especially important when demos vary in palette: infer the relational mapping from each full example before transferring it to a test palette.

Do not derive test outputs by recoloring both input cells independently, selecting whichever input color seems dominant, or guessing from apparent color similarity. Those approaches can produce plausible but wholly incorrect grids. If one or more pair types are not resolved, request a demo only when it is likely to expose those pair types; otherwise, do not spend attempts on unverified mappings. Validate the completed grid's dimensions, row count, and values before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/pair-mapping-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/ray-inference-failure.md", "content": "# Ray extension inference after failed submissions

When a ray-extension prediction is rejected, reject the entire prior generator instead of making a cosmetic grid edit. Re-derive each ray independently from the demos: identify its seed/anchor coordinates, direction vector, color, whether the seed is part of the ray, and the stopping boundary. Track coexisting rays separately; one demo's ray color or direction must not be generalized to all marker groups without evidence. For a test with multiple potential ray seeds, enumerate candidate rays and validate each against every demo, including unchanged markers and endpoints. If the test has a configuration not represented in demonstrations, spend a demo only when it distinguishes explicitly named alternatives. Validate that generated cells do not overwrite given objects unless outputs prove overwrite behavior.
